// The static op types: one MLIR_OP(enumerator, name) line per op
// (doc/mlir_api_design.md, sections 1.2 and 2.5).
//
// This file is an X-macro: define the macros you need, include it, and the
// lines expand through them. Macros left undefined expand to nothing, and
// the file #undefs all of them at the end, so it can be included any number
// of times (it has no include guard on purpose). It generates
//   - the MLIR_OpType enum and its dialect ranges (mlir_api.h),
//   - the name of each type (mlir_op_names.c).
//
// The ops of a dialect sit between MLIR_DIALECT_BEGIN(D) and
// MLIR_DIALECT_END(D), so their values are consecutive and
// MLIR_DIALECT_<D>_FIRST..MLIR_DIALECT_<D>_LAST is a range check
// (MLIR_OP_TYPE_IN_DIALECT in mlir_api.h). Names are unique (checked when a
// context is created). Values are not ABI-stable: everything is rebuilt
// together.
//
// To add an op, add a line to its dialect's block. To add a dialect, add a
// block, and if the dialect exists upstream, load it in
// mlir_api_impl_upstream.cpp (contextRegistry); otherwise upstream treats it
// as an unregistered dialect.

#ifndef MLIR_OP
#define MLIR_OP(enumerator, name)
#endif
#ifndef MLIR_DIALECT_BEGIN
#define MLIR_DIALECT_BEGIN(dialect)
#endif
#ifndef MLIR_DIALECT_END
#define MLIR_DIALECT_END(dialect)
#endif

MLIR_DIALECT_BEGIN(BUILTIN)
// The C side names the module op `module`; upstream calls it `builtin.module`.
MLIR_OP(OP_TYPE_MODULE, "module")
MLIR_OP(OP_TYPE_UNREALIZED_CONVERSION_CAST, "builtin.unrealized_conversion_cast")
MLIR_DIALECT_END(BUILTIN)

// `return` without a dialect prefix, as the in-tree parser reads the custom
// form of a function's return; it belongs to no dialect.
MLIR_OP(OP_TYPE_RETURN, "return")

